ame and the validation result. validate_chain(chain: List[Certificate]) -> tuple[bool, List[str]]:\n40:     \"\"\"Validates a certificate chain by checking issuer/subject continuity.\n41:     \n42:     Args:\n43:         chain: List of certificates from leaf to root.\n43:     \n44:     Returns:\n45:         Tuple of (is_valid, error_messages).\n46:     \"\"\"\n47:     errors = []\n48:     \n49:     for i in range(len(chain) - 1):\n50:         current_cert = chain[i]\n51:         next_cert = chain[i + 1]\n52:         \n53:         if current_cert.issuer != next_cert.subject:\n54:             error_msg = (\n55:                 f\"Chain break at index {i}: \"\n56:                 f\"cert.issuer={current_cert.issuer!r} != \"\n57:                 f\"next.subject={next_cert.subject!r}\"\n58:             )\n59:             errors.append(error_msg)\n60:     \n61:     is_valid = len(errors) == 0\n62:     \n63:     orgops.compliance.record_decision(\n64:         \"tls.chain.validated\",\n64:         is_valid,\n65:         {\"depth\": len(chain), \"errors\": errors if errors else None},\n66:     )\n67:     \n68:     return is_valid, errors\n\nThe `__repr__` method formats the certificate representation. The `ChainValidationError` exception captures validation failures with the index and mismatched issuer/subject details. The main validation function iterates through consecutive certificate pairs, checking that each certificate's issuer matches the next certificate's subject, collecting any breaks in the chain and recording the validation result with depth and error information. validates every adjacent pair\n    for i in range(len(chain) - 1):\n        expected = chain[i].issuer\n        actual = chain[i + 1].subject\n        if expected != actual:\n            errors.append(\n                f\"break at {i}: issuer={expected!r} != subject={actual!r}\"\n            )\n\n    is_valid = len(errors) == 0\n    orgops.compliance.record_decision(\n        \"tls.chain.validated\",\n        is_valid,\n        {\"depth\": len(chain), \"errors\": errors if errors else None},\n    )\n    return {\n        \"valid\": is_valid,\n        \"depth\": len(chain),\n        \"errors\": errors,\n        \"root_self_signed\": _is_self_signed(chain[-1]),\n    }\n\nThe loop now iterates through all adjacent certificate pairs by using `range(len(chain) - 1)`, ensuring the leaf-to-first-intermediate connection is checked. After validation, I'm recording the decision to compliance tracking with the validation result and chain depth, then returning the summary with all errors collected.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":128,"cache_read_input_tokens":10387,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":128},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c7783b7e-9c77-4db4-b3bc-3dea2064becf","uuid":"95474152-75aa-47ef-ba25-9cbf7dd0fc35"}
